now turning to the economy the Federal  Reserve has raised interest rates again  yesterday by three quarters of a  percentage point for the third time this  year is part of a continuing effort to  slow inflation but it could mean a  recession when times like this happened  we lean on one a few people CBS News  business analyst Jill Schlesinger she is  here to break it all down so what does  this mean for consumers there are always  winners and losers so let's start with  the losers borrowers anyone who's  carrying a credit card balance you're  going to get a new auto loan maybe you  have an adjustable rate mortgage and  it's going to adjust higher maybe it's a  small business loan that's all going to  cost you more but on the flip side there  are winners Savers are doing a happy  dance they are finally getting more  money credited to their money market  their savings accounts their uh their  checking accounts and it's been a long  time since we've seen that so for  consumers it is a mixed bag speaking of  time what about the timing why is the  Fed doing this right now well inflation  remains incredibly high so we found out  that we know that overall inflation  Rising by 8.3 percent from a year ago  but every single category internally  Rising food shelter energy and all of  these things eat up so much of a  household's budget the fed's got to  raise rates slow down the economy so  that inflation starts to come down and  so far it has not been successful in  pushing down that inflation rate more  increases to come absolutely in the  fed's projections they're seeing another  1.25 percent before the end of the year  there are two more meetings one in  November one in December additional rate  hikes next year so when we're looking  forward to maybe six eight months from  now we're looking at the FED funds rate  at around 4.6 percent it was at zero to  a quarter of a percent just a year ago  there's people at home that are  wondering are we headed towards uh  recession you say it not me  um look every time the FED raises rates  the likelihood of a potential recession  Rises for sure why because raising  interest rates is a blunt instrument it  takes a while for those interest rate  increases to filter through the economy  and to be clear higher interest rates  are meant to slow things down but they  can slow down too much so most  economists are looking ahead and saying  will likely be in a recession next year  hopefully it is a short and shallow  recession  y'all thank you so much
 

